questiontext.text:
Consider the following code snippet:

```java
class A {
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Class A");
    }
}

class B extends A {
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Class B");
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        A obj = new B();
        obj.display();
    }
}
```

What will be the output of the code?

What is the output of the following code?

```java
class Parent {
    void printMessage() {
        System.out.println("Parent Method");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    void printMessage() {
        System.out.println("Child Method");
    }

    void callParentMethod() {
        super.printMessage();
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Child c = new Child();
        c.printMessage();
        c.callParentMethod();
    }
}
```

Assume there exists the following interface and class:
```java
public interface Fruit {
     public String name();
}

public class Broccoli {
    public String name() {
        <some code>
    }
}
```
Complete the below class so that it conforms to the *Adapter* pattern and acts as an adapter for the **Broccoli** class with the **Fruit** interface as the target.

**Note:** a 0% penalty regime applies. This means that you can click the `Check` button as much as time allows with no penalty.

